/** \page concept The concept of yaxt

  \tableofcontents

  \section intro Introduction
  \section concept Concept

  The usage of yaxt can be divided into six steps:
    - defining global index
    - defining decomposition
    - generating a data type independent mapping between the two decompositions
    - generating a data type dependent redistribution object
    - doing the data exchange(s)
    - cleaning up the generated yaxt objects

  \subsection gindex Defining global index

  Global indices are used in yaxt as an abstraction for the actual data points. The user
  has to assign a unique global index to each unique data point. If a data point occurs
  more than once (e.g. in halo) it is possible to assign the same global index to each
  of these data points.\n
  For example, if the data is a 1D array one could assign the indices 0 to n-1 to the
  elements of the array.\n
  The definion the global index is a theoretically process and has no representation in
  yaxt.

  \subsection decomp Define decomposition

  Once the user is able to assign a global index to each data point,
  each of the data arrays need to be described with respect to the
  data that is available at each process. This is done by using an
  index list (\ref Xt_idxlist). The index list contains for each data
  point that is available on a process the respective global
  index. The order of global indices within the index list needs to
  match the order of data points, which they represent, in the actual
  data array.\n The yaxt library has a varity of methods to generate
  an index list (see \ref idxlists).

  \subsection xmap Generate mapping

  With two different decompositions (source and target) that are defined on all process
  the user can generate an exchange map (\ref Xt_xmap) between them. The exchange map
  matches the source and target decompositions of all processes and generates a mapping
  between source and target points with identical global indices. \n
  It is an error if not all data points of the target decomposition can be matched with
  data points of the source decomposition.\n
  There are multiple algorithms available to generate an exchange map (see \ref xmaps).

  \subsection redist Generate redistribution object

  An exchange map can be used to generate a redistribution object (\ref Xt_redist). Based on the
  MPI data type passed to the constructure of the redistribution object, an object will be created
  that is able to redistribute data of the given type, which is distributed according to the
  source decomposition into the target decomposition. The provided MPI data type allows the
  redistribution of data with an arbitrary data layout.\n
  It is possible to merge multiple redistribution object into a single one (see \ref redist_colls).

  \subsection exchange Do exchange

  The exchange can be executed by a blocking call to \ref xt_redist_s_exchange or
  \ref xt_redist_s_exchange1 (only possible for non-merged redistributions). It can be called
  repeatedly for as many times as necessary.

  \subsection cleanup Clean up

  Once any yaxt object is not directly needed anymore it can be deleted by the respective delete
  calls (e.g. \ref xt_idxlist_delete).
 */
